    <h2>How to judge "quiet" before you buy</h2>
    <p><strong>Ask for a decibel number, not an adjective.</strong> "Quiet," "whisper-quiet," and "low noise" appear on almost every electric aspirator listing, and they mean nothing without a measurement — and a measurement only counts next to the suction level it was taken at. Our 46 dB is SP-1, the Grownsy's lowest setting; a reading at a top setting would be higher, and a reading with no setting attached is not comparable to anything. If a listing gives no number, treat its quietness as unproven — that is exactly how we treat the Momcozy and the Braun here.</p>
    <p><strong>Remember that quiet is not the same as gentle.</strong> A low-decibel motor can still vibrate against a baby's face, and a baby who flinches at vibration will not be comforted by a good dB number. This is one reason the manual NoseFrida keeps winning in our testing: no motor means no vibration at all, and parent-controlled suction means the pressure stops the instant you stop.</p>
    <p><strong>Weigh noise against the 3AM reset.</strong> The quietest electric we measured is also the slowest to clean up in our test — about 120–180 seconds against the NoseFrida's 60–90. A device that is quiet during suction but takes three minutes to rinse, dry, and reassemble while a baby cries is not obviously the better night-time tool. Count the whole job, not just the suction seconds.</p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>What is the quietest electric nasal aspirator?</h3>
    <p>Of the electrics we have actually measured, the GROWNSY: about 46 dB on its lowest setting (SP-1) with a phone decibel meter, roughly the level of a quiet room. The Momcozy BreezyClear and Braun BNA100US are marketed as quiet but untested by us, so we have no sound reading for either and do not rank them on noise. If absolute silence matters most, the manual Frida Baby NoseFrida makes no motor noise at all.</p>
    <h3>Is 46 dB quiet for a baby nasal aspirator?</h3>
    <p>Yes, for a lowest setting. 46 dB is about the level of a quiet room — far below a normal conversation (around 60 dB) and below most household appliances. But remember it is a phone-meter reading on the lowest suction level; higher settings will be louder, and the vibration next to a baby's face matters as much as the number.</p>
    <h3>Are electric nasal aspirators louder than manual ones?</h3>
    <p>Yes, by definition: any electric aspirator has a motor, and any manual aspirator has none. The NoseFrida is completely silent because there is nothing to make noise. The real question is whether the electric you choose is quiet enough on its lowest setting — and for the Grownsy, our measurement says it is.</p>
    <h3>Does a quieter aspirator mean weaker suction?</h3>
    <p>Not necessarily. The Grownsy's SP-1 is both its quietest and its gentlest setting, but it has higher settings for thicker mucus — those are louder. Quietness tracks the setting, not the device's maximum power. A high maximum suction figure, like the Momcozy's 65 kPa, says nothing about how loud the device is at the low setting you will actually use most.</p>

